#
# D-138 — THE READ EDGE, DERIVED OVER THE MODULE RATHER THAN PATCHED AT THE
# SITE THAT REPORTED IT.
#
# THE HARM, driven at ab5a430: append one 0xe9 byte to spawns.log — a log
# foundry itself writes, from this very module — and Foundry-Liveness raises
#
#   UnicodeDecodeError: 'utf-8' codec can't decode byte 0xe9 in position 109
#
# straight across the MCP boundary, naming no file. Foundry-Liveness is
# CT-004 / AC-021 / OT-010's entire surface.
#
# THE FIVE MEMBERS, GUARDED FIVE DIFFERENT WAYS, NONE OF THEM THIS ONE. At
# d3820c5 this scan reported TEN unguarded reads in foundry_spawn.py, not the
# one the defect named:
#
#   _read_progress_ledger:394         except OSError
#   _latest_teammate_dispatches:794   except OSError            <- reported
#   _build_grind_cycle_context:1430   except OSError
#   foundry_spawn_teammate:1319       no handler at all
#   foundry_cast_wave:1615            no handler at all
#   _load_run_state:616               except (OSError, JSONDecodeError)  }
#   _skipped_stream_ids:641           except (OSError, JSONDecodeError)  } D-137,
#   foundry_spawn_teammate:1285       except JSONDecodeError             } casting
#   _build_grind_cycle_context:1468   except (OSError, JSONDecodeError)  } 2's
#   foundry_cast_wave:1575            except JSONDecodeError             } half
#
# Casting 2 closed the last five at ca00b75 by routing them through
# foundry_state.read_json; this casting closed the first five by routing them
# through foundry_state.read_text_file. The scan below is what makes that a
# PROPERTY of the module rather than ten remembered edits: it walks every
# read the file contains and there is no allow-list to enrol a new one in.
#
# WHAT COUNTS AS COVERAGE IS ASKED OF PYTHON. The previous shape of this rule
# elsewhere in the suite compared handler SPELLINGS against a frozenset
# somebody typed, and D-137's structural half is that the table admitted all
# fourteen of its sites because it happened to contain "JSONDecodeError". A
# guard whose own membership table is hand-kept is an instance of the class it
# exists to close, and its failure mode is silent ACCEPTANCE. So handlers are
# resolved to real exception classes through the module's own namespace and
# asked `issubclass`; a spelling the resolver cannot see is REPORTED rather
# than skipped.
#
# THE PLANTED BLOCK is the rule's own adjacent path: a fresh member in each of
# the five shapes this module has actually worn, plus one written correctly.
# `properly_guarded` is absent from the offender list on its own merits — the
# thing it is guarded by is its own try/except, not an entry on a list.
#
# GRIND-21 / D-146 — THE SAME RULE, NOW OVER THE SHIPPED SOURCE. The scan
# above was bound to `Path(fs.__file__)`: one module, derived over its every
# read, and blind to every other module in the package. Its three sibling
# structural rules all derived membership over `_package_modules`; this one did
# not, and PROVE proved the difference by planting an unguarded `read_text` in
# a NEW subpackage, in foundry_validate.py and in foundry_orchestrator.py, all
# three GREEN against the full 1756-test suite. Membership now comes from the
# same `_scanned_roots` rglob the manifest rule uses, over both shipped trees.
#
# THE PREDICATE IS DERIVED TOO, AND THAT IS THE HALF THE CLASS RETREATED INTO.
# Going package-wide with `open` as a bare NAME reported 41 sites, of which
# eighteen cannot decode a byte: append-only log writes, a `"rb"` handle, and
# `"a+"` handles opened only to hold an flock. So each axis is asked of Python
# rather than read off the source's punctuation —
#
#   which stream a mode yields   a scratch file is opened in that very mode and
#                                the object inspected: isinstance(h,
#                                io.TextIOBase) and h.readable()
#   whether an errors= handler   the handler is fetched from the codecs
#   raises                       registry and handed a real UnicodeDecodeError
#   whether an attribute reads   it is called on a text handle over an
#                                undecodable byte and watched
#   where an argument sits       inspect.signature, because open(p,"r") puts
#                                the mode at index 1 and p.open("r") at index 0
#
# — and a mode or errors= value that is not a literal is REPORTED and counted
# as a member, never skipped.
#
# The module's own two spawns.log APPENDS therefore leave this rule: they are
# mode "a", which Python says is a text stream that is not readable. The scan
# is anchored on foundry_state.py#read_text_file instead — the guarded
# primitive, and the one `read_text` in the package that cannot legitimately
# disappear — so a matcher that stops recognising `read_text` fails BY NAME
# rather than reporting a clean package (D-142).
#
